+### *** WHAT MAY ENTER THIS BLOCK: A PREMISE IS SETTLED **IN A SCOPE**, AND CARRYING IT OUT OF THAT
+### SCOPE MAKES IT AN UNVERIFIED CLAIM AGAIN.  Minted 2026-09-11 from FOUR instances in ONE DAY. ***
+
+Every one was a claim verified correctly somewhere, then reused somewhere else without re-checking:
+
+| # | the claim | where it was true | where it was reused | cost |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | the clause-(d) flow test | `tlb_mode` -> `tileptr` | **stopped at the ARRAY, never reached the INDEX** `tile_nr` | six measured escapes; a round re-opened after it was ready to close |
+| 2 | *"`color_mode` flows into no lvalue the guarded body reads"* | the edited `default:` arm | **its sibling accept arms, which fill `buf`** | a false warrant shipped into a contract and reused in the same document |
+| 3 | *"the conditional must precede the loop"* | `X(rlwimi)`'s handler | **one call earlier, in the decoder** | an adopted repair that would have been INERT |
+| 4 | *"`odata` is computed before the offset is examined"* | `dev_wdc.c` | **`dev_dreamcast_maple.c`, which does not share it** | a settled-premises block that was wrong about the device under review |
+
+**NOT ONE was caught by re-reading my own reasoning.** Three were caught by seats and one by opening
+the file. That is the whole argument for this block existing -- and it is also the argument for what
+now governs entry to it:
+
+> ### **A PREMISE INHERITED FROM A SIBLING INVESTIGATION -- ANOTHER FUNCTION, ANOTHER DEVICE, ANOTHER
+> ### CALL LEVEL, ANOTHER ARM OF THE SAME SWITCH -- IS **NOT SETTLED HERE** UNTIL IT HAS BEEN RE-READ
+> ### IN THIS SCOPE.  Cite the line you re-read, or state it as a QUESTION instead.**
+
+**Why this is the dangerous kind and not merely another wrong fact:** a premise from a sibling
+investigation **arrives already believed**. It has a provenance, it was checked, and the checking was
+real -- so it passes the reader's guard precisely because the reader remembers verifying it. The
+four above were all *true sentences*; what was false was their address.
+
+> ### *** AND CORRECT THE BRIEF IT CAME FROM, IN THE SAME EDIT.  A TABLE ROW HERE CLOSES THE
+> ### CLASS FOR FUTURE BRIEFS AND LEAVES THE ORIGINATING BRIEF ON DISK, LOADED AND RE-FIREABLE.
+> ### Measured 2026-09-11, on row 4 of the table above. ***
+
+Row 4 was recorded correctly, naming the device and the premise.  **The brief it came from was never touched** -- and months later it was fired again, twice in one session, still carrying the refuted premise as SETTLED.  The seat caught the same error a second time, from the source.
+
+> **A TEMPLATE GOVERNS BRIEFS WRITTEN *FROM* IT.  IT DOES NOT REACH BRIEFS ALREADY WRITTEN.**
+
+*This file is the piece of prose the carrier holds up as the one that demonstrably works, because it lives where the decision happens.  It does -- for a brief being WRITTEN.  The decision to RE-FIRE an existing brief happens somewhere else, and nothing was watching there.*
+
+**The habit is one line: when you add a row to the table above, open the brief named in its "where it was reused" column and correct the premise IN PLACE, quoting what it refutes.**  If the brief is gone, say so in the row -- an absent brief and an uncorrected one must not render the same.
+
+*The cheap habit: when a settled premise names a FILE or a FUNCTION, re-grep it in the file this brief
+is about, and put the line number in the premise. A premise carrying its own citation cannot be about
+the wrong device -- and a premise that resists being cited is one you are remembering, not reading.*
+
+### *** VERIFY EVERY PERMITTED PATH EXISTS BEFORE THE BRIEF IS FIRED.  ONE `ls`.
+### Minted 2026-09-11 after I invented TWO filenames in ONE DAY, in briefs to the same seat. ***
+
+| # | the path I wrote | where it came from | what it actually is |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `src/devices/dev_crime.c` | the row id `crimewidthkill` + the constructor `dev_crime_init()` | **no such file anywhere under the project root**; the code is `dev_sgi_ip32.c`, `DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)` -- **and the row's own TITLE says so in its first three words** |
+| 2 | `regress/sh_tlb_probe.py` | the row's subject (SH TLB protection) | **no such file anywhere**, including ignored files and link traversal; the real probe is `sh_tlbmulti_probe.py` |
+
+**BOTH were derived from what the row is ABOUT rather than from what the row SAYS.**  A row id, a constructor name and a header name are three pointers to a filename that need not exist -- and when all three agree, the invented name looks *more* plausible, not less.
+
+> **THE SEAT CAUGHT BOTH AND REFUSED TO SUBSTITUTE A NEIGHBOUR**, saying in the second case that other TLB filenames do not establish which file the detector is.  **That refusal is the correct behaviour and it cost a whole invocation each time** -- the seat spent its answer explaining what it could not check.
+
+**THE RULE: before firing, run the permitted-files list through `ls` and paste nothing that does not come back.**  It is one command and it cannot be forgotten in the way a resolution can:
+
+```
+for f in <every path in the READ ONLY THESE FILES block>; do
+    [ -e "$f" ] || echo "MISSING: $f"
+done
+```
+
+*The second incident is the instructive one: the row it belonged to ALSO cites no artifact for its own headline measurement, so the invented path was filling a real hole rather than replacing a real name.  **A brief that has to invent a path is often a brief whose ROW is missing one** -- check the row before inventing the file.*
